DevOps Interview Experience – Senior DevOps Engineer #Capgemini
Recently, I went through a 3-round interview process for a Senior DevOps Engineer role. Sharing my experience — hope this helps someone preparing for similar roles.
🔹 Round 1 (L1 – Technical | 30 Minutes)
The interviewer was polite and punctual.
We started with:
Tell me about yourself
Explain your day-to-day responsibilities
Walk me through your project architecture end-to-end
Then the discussion moved into Kubernetes:
Explain Kubernetes architecture and request flow
Difference between Taints & Node Affinity
How Load Balancer works in Kubernetes (end-to-end setup)
Real production issues faced in K8s and how I resolved them
CI/CD & Security:
End-to-end Jenkins pipeline flow
How I secure pipelines
Credential management and storage
Docker:
Why multi-stage Dockerfile?
Basic Docker commands
Terraform:
Remote state management
Folder structure
State handling
Other topics:
Ansible usage and real-world scenarios
Monitoring tools and setup
After 2 days, I was shortlisted for L2.
🔹 Round 2 (L2 – Senior Manager | Scenario-Based | 30 Minutes)
This round focused more on real-world scenarios and leadership.
Questions included:
Biggest challenges in your DevOps career and how you handled them
Team structure and my role (individual contributor vs leadership)
Handling production pressure
Deep dive into:
Kubernetes architecture (end-to-end explanation)
Securing Kubernetes clusters
Managing secrets and credentials
Cloud:
AWS services used
Openness to multi-cloud
Practical questions:
Write a simple Dockerfile
Python: Program to find max number in a list
Ansible-related scenarios
After 2–3 days, I was informed I cleared L2.
🔹 HR Round
Standard HR discussion:
Why do you want to join?
Career goals
Past challenges
Compensation discussion
Final Verdict: ✅ Selected & Offer Received.
Key Takeaways
 1️⃣ Interviews are not just about tools — they are about clarity of thought.
 2️⃣ Scenario-based questions matter more at senior levels.
 3️⃣ Listen carefully to the interviewer before answering.
 4️⃣ Explain with structure — not random points.
 5️⃣ Real production experience always stands out.
💻 When preparing, don’t just revise commands.
Understand architecture, failure handling, security, and decision-making.
Hope this helps someone preparing for DevOps interviews.
